// THE "MITHRAS LITURGY" (PGM IV 475–829) IN G. R. S. MEAD, A MITHRIAC RITUAL
// (ECHOES FROM THE GNOSIS VOL. VI; LONDON: THEOSOPHICAL PUBLISHING SOCIETY, 1907).
//
// The text comes from mithras-liturgy-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/mead-mithriac-ritual.py from the archive.org OCR of three
// independent scans of the 1907 book (the University of Toronto copy, a Google
// scan of a copy in an unnamed library, and the Servants of Knowledge scan of a
// copy in Bangalore), voted word by word and then read page by page (pp. 9–33)
// against the page images of the Toronto copy. Every correction made by hand is
// listed in the script. Imported whole: Mead's Preamble (pp. 9–17) and the
// Ritual (pp. 18–33) in his fourteen sections. Listed, not imported: his
// Comments (pp. 34–77).
//
// WHAT THE TEXT IS. A Greek ritual of ascent and immortalisation, preserved in
// one witness, the Great Paris Magical Papyrus (Bibliothèque nationale de
// France, Supplément grec 574), a book of the fourth century CE. Albrecht
// Dieterich edited it in 1903 as "Eine Mithrasliturgie"; Mead translated it
// from Dieterich's text, leaving out the magical names and vowel-strings that
// he took for later insertions, except those marked by obeli (†) and the one
// block of vowels he prints (VII.2). Mead's English is a translation of a
// translator's selection, and his Preamble reads the rite in Theosophical
// terms ("a yoga-rite", "Ātmic Energy"); the archive records that as his.
//
// DATES. The papyrus is dated by its holding library ("IVe siècle", on the
// labels photographed for Commons) and by Mead ("the earliest years of the
// fourth century A.D."). The date of composition is debated: the only dating
// given here is Dieterich's, as Mead reports it (made in Egypt "about 100-150
// A.D."), and it is shown as one scholar's view. Whether the Ritual is Mithraic
// at all is disputed: Mead says Cumont rejected it "as a genuine Mithriac
// document".
//
// THE LINKS are proposed and say what they rest on. Mead translated both this
// Ritual and the Hermetica the archive holds, so where the English of the two
// agrees ("reborn in Mind") the agreement may be his; no link claims that one
// text knew the other.
//
// Nothing is marked verified: the text is a careful copy, read against the page
// images, not checked against the papyrus or Dieterich's Greek.

